From b27e1b17dc92c899464e5da9b96eb8056bb39d23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FNGarvin <184324400+FNGarvin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 22:37:54 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] Write media dumps via the parent process instead of
 requiring a disabled sandbox

Adds PContent's RequestMediaDumpFile(path) returns (FileDescOrError), a
new async IPC round trip modeled directly on the existing
RequestAnonymousTemporaryFile/ProvideAnonymousTemporaryFile pattern
(dom/ipc/ContentParent.cpp) and the CreateAudioIPCConnection "returns"
call shape -- except it opens a caller-chosen named path (creating it,
truncating if it exists) rather than an anonymous temp file, since the
whole point here is a user-picked, persistent destination.

The parent opens the file on an IO-capable thread and hands back a
duplicated handle; the content side imports it back into a PRFileDesc via
PR_ImportFile(), the same conversion ContentChild::RecvProvideAnonymousTemporaryFile
already does for the temp-file case.

MediaDumpSink now requests this handle once, asynchronously, instead of
calling fopen()/PR_Open() directly on a path the content-process sandbox
would normally block. Since that request is an unavoidable async
round-trip, writes that arrive before the handle does are buffered (in
order) and flushed once it arrives. Falls back to opening the path
directly when there's no ContentChild singleton (i.e. running in the
parent process itself, which has no sandbox restriction to route around).

MOZ_DISABLE_CONTENT_SANDBOX is no longer required for this feature, though
the run script still sets it for now as a safety net until this has seen
more real-world testing.
